# No trailing slash: `node_modules/` matches a directory and nothing else, so a
# `node_modules` that is a *symlink* — which is what `git worktree` setups and some CI
# caches leave behind — sails past it and can be committed by `git add -A`. One did, and
# every job on the PR died in twelve seconds with
# `ENOTDIR: not a directory, mkdir .../node_modules` out of `pnpm install`, because pnpm
# cannot make a directory where a symlink already is.
node_modules
.next/
dist/
build/
coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo

.env
.env.*
!.env.example

.DS_Store
*.log
.seed-*.log
.loop-logs/

playwright-report/
test-results/
out/
next-env.d.ts

# The responsive matrix (#1891). `pnpm responsive <slug>` writes thirty-three screenshots
# per game per engine: a picture of one run on one machine, not a baseline anybody compares
# against. The committed pictures are `e2e/__screenshots__` and nothing else (#227); these
# are for attaching to the issue the run was made for.
responsive-matrix/

# The dev server keeps its own build output so a production build cannot clobber it.
apps/web/.next-dev/

# The visual baselines (#227) are tracked, but only the platform CI compares on. macOS
# rasterises text through CoreText and Linux through the FreeType inside Chromium, so the
# same page is a different picture on each and a committed `-darwin` baseline could only
# ever be an image nothing checks. `e2e/visual.spec.ts` skips rather than fails off Linux
# and `-u` there writes these, which are for watching your own change and nobody else's.
e2e/__screenshots__/*-darwin.png
e2e/__screenshots__/*-win32.png

# The share images (#2453). `pnpm share:images` composes all 109 of them from
# apps/web/src/lib/tiles.ts before every build, so they are output and not source — and
# tracking them would mean 109 assets.license.json entries recording one piece of
# provenance a hundred and nine times. apps/web/assets.license.json licenses the code that
# draws them, which is the same answer rule 3 already takes for the catalogue tiles.
apps/web/public/og/
.lighthouseci/
